1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,427
Today is September 16, 2026.

3
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:14,412
The S&P 500 closed down zero point four five percent at seven thousand five hundred fifty-one.

4
00:00:14,412 --> 00:00:17,525
It lost its sixty-day moving average.

5
00:00:17,525 --> 00:00:22,807
The prior high at seven thousand six hundred twenty was reclaimed, then lost.

6
00:00:22,807 --> 00:00:28,322
Nasdaq was down just one hundredth of a percent, barely holding its sixty-day line.

7
00:00:28,322 --> 00:00:31,949
The tape has been grinding sideways for nearly a quarter.

8
00:00:31,949 --> 00:00:33,726
No direction.

9
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:35,491
No leadership.

10
00:00:35,491 --> 00:00:37,255
No momentum.

11
00:00:37,255 --> 00:00:43,076
The Fed hiked twenty-five basis points to a range of three and three-quarters to four percent.

12
00:00:43,076 --> 00:00:44,817
Unanimous.

13
00:00:44,817 --> 00:00:46,826
Zero dissents.

14
00:00:46,826 --> 00:00:51,312
This is the first rate increase since July of two thousand thirteen.

15
00:00:51,312 --> 00:00:54,596
Chair Warsh kept his presser under eight minutes.

16
00:00:54,596 --> 00:00:57,268
He hammered the inflation message.

17
00:00:57,268 --> 00:00:59,976
The tape sold off in real time.

18
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:02,623
The dot plot is the real story.

19
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:07,868
Sixteen of eighteen voters see one more twenty-five basis point hike this year.

20
00:01:07,868 --> 00:01:11,986
The twenty-six year-end median sits at four and one-tenths.

21
00:01:11,986 --> 00:01:16,606
Twenty-seven holds at four and one-tenths before the cycle turns down.

22
00:01:16,606 --> 00:01:21,140
Inflation hitting the two percent target is now pushed to twenty-nine.

23
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:25,135
The ten-year Treasury yield jumped to four point nine seven.

24
00:01:25,135 --> 00:01:28,554
The thirty-year is at five point three five.

25
00:01:28,554 --> 00:01:30,527
Twenty-year highs.

26
00:01:30,527 --> 00:01:36,741
The last time we saw this level of bond stress was the two-thousand dot-com crash and September eleventh.

27
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:41,630
August retail sales came in up zero point eight percent month over month.

28
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:44,645
Core control group up one point four.

29
00:01:44,645 --> 00:01:46,863
Strong on the surface.

30
00:01:46,863 --> 00:01:50,050
But real wages have been falling for months.

31
00:01:50,050 --> 00:01:53,003
Household savings are being drawn down.

32
00:01:53,003 --> 00:01:55,564
This is wealth-effect consumption.

33
00:01:55,564 --> 00:01:58,040
Not durable income growth.

34
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:01,814
Now, here is where the thesis genuinely splits.

35
00:02:01,814 --> 00:02:07,010
On one hand, the consensus view is that this tightening cycle is near its tail.

36
00:02:07,010 --> 00:02:10,552
The Fed has only two or three more hikes left.

37
00:02:10,552 --> 00:02:16,238
Goldman's forward-looking inflation indicators show pressure below two-thousand eighteen levels.

38
00:02:16,238 --> 00:02:21,422
Core CPI has already drifted from two point nine down to two point four.

39
00:02:21,422 --> 00:02:24,130
The hike accelerates the decline.

40
00:02:24,130 --> 00:02:26,189
It does not reverse it.

41
00:02:26,189 --> 00:02:30,233
Under that framing, this is a one-time repricing event.

42
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:33,248
Not the start of a sustained bear market.

43
00:02:33,248 --> 00:02:41,545
On the other hand, Wells Fargo just cut its S&P year-end target from seven thousand nine hundred fifty to seven thousand seven hundred.

44
00:02:41,545 --> 00:02:44,106
They are calling this cycle tail.

45
00:02:44,106 --> 00:02:47,427
The main driver is no longer earnings growth.

46
00:02:47,427 --> 00:02:49,706
It is multiple compression.

47
00:02:49,706 --> 00:02:53,689
Liquidity indicators are at year-to-date lows through November.

48
00:02:53,689 --> 00:02:57,047
High-beta growth stocks are the most exposed.

49
00:02:57,047 --> 00:02:59,572
And then there is the third camp.

50
00:02:59,572 --> 00:03:02,096
The midterm election year pattern.

51
00:03:02,096 --> 00:03:05,969
September and October are historically the weakest window.

52
00:03:05,969 --> 00:03:12,427
But since nineteen seventy-four, the six months after the election have produced gains every single time.

53
00:03:12,427 --> 00:03:15,895
Average return, twelve point four percent.

54
00:03:15,895 --> 00:03:18,309
Twice the historical norm.

55
00:03:18,309 --> 00:03:21,263
And it does not matter which party wins.

56
00:03:21,263 --> 00:03:26,030
Under that framework, this pullback is the last buying window of the year.

57
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:28,922
The earnings data supports the bulls.

58
00:03:28,922 --> 00:03:33,407
Second quarter corporate profits grew forty-seven point four percent.

59
00:03:33,407 --> 00:03:38,285
Strip out big tech investment gains, you still get twenty-eight point eight.

60
00:03:38,285 --> 00:03:43,224
That is the strongest print since the post-crisis low base in two thousand nine.

61
00:03:43,224 --> 00:03:46,741
Third quarter is tracking to twenty-nine point two.

62
00:03:46,741 --> 00:03:49,841
Fourth quarter, twenty-four point three.

63
00:03:49,841 --> 00:03:53,800
And the forward P and E sits around nineteen times.

64
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:55,895
That is not stretched.

65
00:03:55,895 --> 00:03:59,020
But the bears have a point on the bond side.

66
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:02,942
Deutsche Bank is calling this a mild re-tightening regime.

67
00:04:02,942 --> 00:04:10,944
The historical pattern says long-end yields keep climbing fifty to a hundred basis points in the year after a new hiking cycle begins.

68
00:04:10,944 --> 00:04:16,704
The only exception was nineteen ninety-seven, when the Asian crisis forced an immediate pivot.

69
00:04:16,704 --> 00:04:21,030
The AI capital boom has structurally raised the neutral rate.

70
00:04:21,030 --> 00:04:25,160
The economy can absorb higher rates without a sharp recession.

71
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:28,885
That is the Fed's implicit argument for staying hawkish.

72
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,234
JPMorgan offers a useful framework.

73
00:04:32,184 --> 00:04:35,174
The question is not whether rates rise.

74
00:04:35,174 --> 00:04:38,409
It is whether the curve steepens or flattens.

75
00:04:38,409 --> 00:04:41,436
Steepening means the economy is strong.

76
00:04:41,436 --> 00:04:44,022
Energy and financials lead.

77
00:04:44,022 --> 00:04:47,270
Flattening means policy is choking growth.

78
00:04:47,270 --> 00:04:51,020
Quality tech with stable cash flows holds up better.

79
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:56,375
The real danger is disorderly rate spikes hitting at the same time as earnings roll over.

80
00:04:56,375 --> 00:04:58,949
That is the scenario to watch.

81
00:04:58,949 --> 00:05:03,642
Let me flag the five overlapping risks compressing the window right now.

82
00:05:03,642 --> 00:05:08,409
First, the Fed hike and the uncertainty around how many more are coming.

83
00:05:08,409 --> 00:05:13,875
Second, Treasury yields at two-decade highs, pulling capital out of equities.

84
00:05:13,875 --> 00:05:18,569
Third, AI data center construction is becoming a political issue.

85
00:05:18,569 --> 00:05:22,625
Over half of Americans oppose a data center in their neighborhood.

86
00:05:22,625 --> 00:05:25,799
Both parties will use it as a campaign weapon.

87
00:05:25,799 --> 00:05:27,956
Approvals will slow.

88
00:05:27,956 --> 00:05:31,657
But neither party will actually block AI progress.

89
00:05:31,657 --> 00:05:35,505
Fourth, the US-China summit at the end of the month.

90
00:05:35,505 --> 00:05:39,647
Trade friction will flare before the meeting, cool down after.

91
00:05:39,647 --> 00:05:43,017
It is a timing risk, not a structural one.

92
00:05:43,017 --> 00:05:53,336
The two-thousand twenty-four September precedent, where a summit preceded a single-day three point six percent selloff, was really an AI investment scare wearing a trade mask.

93
00:05:53,336 --> 00:05:56,828
Fifth, the Anthropic IPO in October.

94
00:05:56,828 --> 00:05:59,831
That is a pure capital siphon event.

95
00:05:59,831 --> 00:06:04,206
Bigger than the SpaceX listing in terms of institutional appetite.

96
00:06:04,206 --> 00:06:07,037
But it is a one-time liquidity drain.

97
00:06:07,037 --> 00:06:09,353
Not a fundamental shift.

98
00:06:09,353 --> 00:06:15,529
None of these five, individually or combined, meet the threshold for a systemic bear market.

99
00:06:15,529 --> 00:06:22,539
The fast-hiking scenario, like two thousand twenty or nineteen ninety-nine, is extremely low probability.

100
00:06:22,539 --> 00:06:26,963
The slow-hike, pause, observe path is the base case.

101
00:06:26,963 --> 00:06:28,863
On the watchlist.

102
00:06:28,863 --> 00:06:33,397
SpaceX is up five percent today to one fifty point eight eight.

103
00:06:33,397 --> 00:06:36,350
The catalyst is September twenty-second.

104
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:38,814
Starship flight fourteen.

105
00:06:38,814 --> 00:06:41,596
First attempt at a full Earth orbit.

106
00:06:41,596 --> 00:06:45,272
Six laps, then splashdown in the Pacific.

107
00:06:45,272 --> 00:06:49,941
Twenty-six Starlink V3 satellites deployed in a single pass.

108
00:06:49,941 --> 00:06:54,586
That is twenty-six terabytes of new network capacity in one shot.

109
00:06:54,586 --> 00:06:57,392
Commercialization is accelerating.

110
00:06:57,392 --> 00:07:03,642
Intel is up four percent on talks with SK Hynix to produce memory chips on US soil.

111
00:07:03,642 --> 00:07:06,792
Lease capacity or form a joint venture.

112
00:07:06,792 --> 00:07:08,924
No final deal yet.

113
00:07:08,924 --> 00:07:11,424
But the optionality is real.

114
00:07:11,424 --> 00:07:16,154
Nvidia and Tesla both sit near their two-hundred-day moving averages.

115
00:07:16,154 --> 00:07:18,569
Those are the line in the sand.

116
00:07:18,569 --> 00:07:21,914
Below that, the technical structure breaks.

117
00:07:21,914 --> 00:07:25,346
Above it, the range-bound chop continues.

118
00:07:25,346 --> 00:07:28,679
The S&P has one clear support level.

119
00:07:28,679 --> 00:07:31,216
Seven thousand four hundred.

120
00:07:31,216 --> 00:07:35,162
If that gives, the next leg down opens up fast.

121
00:07:35,162 --> 00:07:41,865
Resistance is the lost high at seven thousand six hundred twenty, then the sixty-day average that just broke.

122
00:07:41,865 --> 00:07:46,522
For the crypto corner, the stablecoin bill failed its procedural vote.

123
00:07:46,522 --> 00:07:49,451
No favorable legislation this year.

124
00:07:49,451 --> 00:07:52,551
Circle and Coinbase took a two-day hit.

125
00:07:52,551 --> 00:07:57,172
But the bill not passing does not reduce their existing revenue streams.

126
00:07:57,172 --> 00:08:00,100
Circle is trading on pure sentiment.

127
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:02,355
Extreme volatility.

128
00:08:02,355 --> 00:08:04,390
Emotional pricing.

129
00:08:04,390 --> 00:08:06,485
One more data point.

130
00:08:06,485 --> 00:08:18,115
The New Zealand superannuation fund, five hundred forty-four billion dollars in assets, ten-year annualized return of nine point six eight, has already cut its US equity risk exposure.

131
00:08:18,115 --> 00:08:20,652
They see the rally as stretched.

132
00:08:20,652 --> 00:08:28,115
The triggers they flagged: high-level rate hikes, AI giants signaling a slowdown, and the rally being overextended.

133
00:08:28,115 --> 00:08:31,167
That is a meaningful institutional signal.

134
00:08:31,167 --> 00:08:34,218
Key dates to track this week and next.

135
00:08:34,218 --> 00:08:38,140
September twenty-second, Bank of England rate decision.

136
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:42,196
September twenty-second, Bank of Japan rate decision.

137
00:08:42,196 --> 00:08:50,346
September twenty-ninth, the triple expiry of global futures, equity index futures, and single-stock futures contracts.

138
00:08:50,346 --> 00:08:54,022
That is a volatility event regardless of direction.

139
00:08:54,022 --> 00:08:57,000
And the ongoing Middle East situation.

140
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,404
The US strategic petroleum reserve is at its lowest level since nineteen eighty-two.

141
00:09:02,404 --> 00:09:05,615
One more escalation and there is no cushion.

142
00:09:05,615 --> 00:09:09,623
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

143
00:09:09,623 --> 00:09:13,789
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
